I guess that your suggestion does enforce a ratio as well.
I do think that it doesn't go far enough -- people will figure
out the optimal number of A to dip into B to maximize the number
of potions they create.  I haven't done the math, but I'd guess
that 10:1 ratios would be about expected.  I tend to think that
ratios closer to 2:1 would probably be better.  It also means
that (with color alchemy) if object detection or another easily-
obtained potion is part of a recipe to make something useful,
that doesn't automatically mean you can make huge numbers of
them, because you still have to find reasonable numbers of
the second ingredient.

The other reason why I kind of like a hard cap is from the
point of view of what makes sense.  It seems kind of odd
to me that 25 potions of A + 1 potion of B produces the same
result as 25 potions of B + 1 potion of A.  Making the potions
explode (sometimes) when trying to dilute at high ratios doesn't 
really seem logical; while requiring that the ratios are close
to 1:1 to create an effective result seems reasonable.  When
possible, I think it's best that the rules have logical sense
to them, so that players that aren't totally spoiled won't
be completely surprised.


The main thing I like about the color alchemy idea is that
it can encourage people to experiment with their potions --
it adds a new aspect to the game for those who would like
to play with it.  Right now, there's not really any good
reason to mix potions unless you already know exactly what
the result is going to be.  It would be nice if there was
a good reason to do things like mix up your worthless
potions and see what results you get.

